# Kubernetes Admission Controllers — Complete Notes and Hands-on Labs

## 1. Introduction

A Kubernetes Admission Controller is a component that intercepts requests sent to the Kubernetes API Server after authentication and authorization but before the requested object is stored in etcd.

Admission Controllers act as security and policy checkpoints inside the Kubernetes API Server.

They help Kubernetes decide whether a resource should be accepted, modified, or rejected.

### Kubernetes API Request Flow

```text
kubectl apply / kubectl create
            |
            v
     Kubernetes API Server
            |
            v
      Authentication
       (Who are you?)
            |
            v
       Authorization
    (Are you allowed?)
            |
            v
    Admission Controllers
     /              \
    v                v
Mutating          Validating
    \                /
     v              v
       Allow / Deny
            |
            v
           etcd
```

### Authentication vs Authorization vs Admission Control

| Component | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Authentication | Who are you? |
| Authorization | What are you allowed to do? |
| Admission Controller | Should this request be modified, accepted, or rejected? |

## 2. Role of Admission Controllers

Admission Controllers act as gatekeepers for the Kubernetes API.

They can:

- Validate requests and check whether resources follow policies.
- Mutate requests by automatically adding or changing fields.
- Reject requests that violate security or resource policies.
- Enforce consistent configurations across namespaces.
- Improve security, compliance, and resource management.

Admission control applies to Kubernetes API requests from kubectl, CI/CD pipelines, operators, controllers, and applications.

## 3. Types of Admission Controllers

Kubernetes supports built-in admission controllers and dynamically configured admission webhooks.

### Built-in Admission Controllers

These are included in Kubernetes and configured through the API Server.

| Controller | Purpose |
|---|---|
| NamespaceLifecycle | Validates namespace-related operations |
| LimitRanger | Applies default resource requests and limits |
| ResourceQuota | Enforces namespace resource quotas |
| PodSecurity | Enforces Pod Security Standards |
| DefaultStorageClass | Assigns default storage classes |

### Dynamic Admission Controllers

Dynamic admission webhooks allow external services to participate in admission decisions.

| Type | Purpose |
|---|---|
| MutatingAdmissionWebhook | Modifies API objects |
| ValidatingAdmissionWebhook | Accepts or rejects API requests |

### Common Kubernetes Policy and Security Tools

| Tool | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Kyverno | Kubernetes-native policy management |
| OPA Gatekeeper | Policy enforcement and governance |
| Istio | Service mesh with optional sidecar injection |
| Falco | Runtime security monitoring |

Note: Falco is primarily a runtime threat-detection tool, not an admission controller.

