# LAB 4 — Dynamic Admission Controllers with Kyverno

## 13. What is Kyverno?

Kyverno is a Kubernetes-native policy engine.

It helps teams manage Kubernetes policies without writing custom admission webhook applications.

Kyverno can:

- Validate Kubernetes resources.
- Mutate resources automatically.
- Generate resources.
- Enforce security and configuration standards.
- Apply governance policies across clusters.

### Kyverno Workflow

```text
Developer
    |
    v
kubectl apply
    |
    v
Kubernetes API Server
    |
    v
Kyverno Admission Webhook
    |
    v
Evaluate Policy
    |
    +---- Validate
    |
    +---- Mutate
    |
    v
Allow / Reject / Modify
    |
    v
etcd
```

## 14. Install Helm

Download Helm:

```bash
curl -fsSL -o get_helm.sh \
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm/main/scripts/get-helm-3
```

Make the installer executable:

```bash
chmod 700 get_helm.sh
```

Run the installer:

```bash
./get_helm.sh
```

Verify:

```bash
helm version
```

## 15. Install Kyverno

Add the Helm repository:

```bash
helm repo add kyverno https://kyverno.github.io/kyverno/
```

Update repositories:

```bash
helm repo update
```

Install Kyverno:

```bash
helm install kyverno kyverno/kyverno \
  -n kyverno \
  --create-namespace
```

Check Pods:

```bash
kubectl get pods -n kyverno
```

For production, use a supported Kyverno version and follow its high-availability installation recommendations.

## 16. Kyverno Validation Policy

### Objective

Reject Pods that do not have an `env` label.

Example valid labels:

```text
env=dev
env=prod
env=test
```

### Create a policy

File: `require-env-label.yml`

```yaml
apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy
metadata:
  name: require-env-label
spec:
  validationFailureAction: Enforce
  rules:
    - name: check-env-label
      match:
        resources:
          kinds:
            - Pod
      validate:
        message: "Pod must have an 'env' label"
        pattern:
          metadata:
            labels:
              env: "?*"
```

### Apply the policy

```bash
kubectl apply -f require-env-label.yml
```

### Test without a label

```bash
kubectl run testpod --image=nginx
```

Expected result:

The request is rejected because the Pod does not contain the required `env` label.

### Test with a label

```bash
kubectl run testpod --image=nginx --labels=env=dev
```

Expected result:

The request is allowed.

### Cleanup

```bash
kubectl delete pod testpod --ignore-not-found
kubectl delete -f require-env-label.yml
```

### Important Version Note

This example uses the legacy `kyverno.io/v1` ClusterPolicy API.

Recent Kyverno versions warn that this API is deprecated.

For new projects, use the appropriate current Kyverno policy API, such as ValidatingPolicy, and check the installed Kyverno version.

Official migration reference:

https://kyverno.io/docs/guides/migration-to-cel/

## 17. Kyverno Mutation Policy

### Objective

Automatically add the label:

```text
environment: production
```

to newly created Pods.

### Create mutation policy

File: `add-label-policy.yml`

```yaml
apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy
metadata:
  name: add-default-label
spec:
  rules:
    - name: add-env-label
      match:
        resources:
          kinds:
            - Pod
      mutate:
        patchStrategicMerge:
          metadata:
            labels:
              environment: production
```

### Apply the policy

```bash
kubectl apply -f add-label-policy.yml
```

### Create a Pod

```bash
kubectl run nginx --image=nginx
```

### Verify the injected label

```bash
kubectl get pod nginx --show-labels
```

Or:

```bash
kubectl get pod nginx -o yaml | grep environment
```

Or:

```bash
kubectl describe pod nginx | grep production
```

Expected label:

```yaml
labels:
  environment: production
```

### Mutation Workflow

```text
kubectl run nginx
       |
       v
Kubernetes API Server
       |
       v
Kyverno Mutating Webhook
       |
       v
Checks Mutation Policy
       |
       v
Adds environment: production
       |
       v
Kubernetes Creates Modified Pod
```

### Cleanup

```bash
kubectl delete pod nginx --ignore-not-found
kubectl delete -f add-label-policy.yml
```

### Lab Result

Kyverno automatically modifies the incoming Pod configuration before the object is stored.

This is an example of a Mutating Admission Controller.

