# 19. Control Plane, Kubelet, and Static Pods

## Does the Kubernetes Control Plane have a Kubelet?

Yes, in common self-managed Kubernetes installations.

The control-plane machine is also a Kubernetes node and normally runs a kubelet.

In kubeadm-based clusters, the kubelet manages several control-plane components as static Pods.

### Typical kubeadm Architecture

```text
Control Plane Node
        |
        v
      Kubelet
        |
        v
Static Pod Manifests
        |
        +---- kube-apiserver
        |
        +---- kube-controller-manager
        |
        +---- kube-scheduler
        |
        +---- etcd (stacked topology)
```

The kubelet watches the static Pod manifest directory and ensures the specified Pods are running.

### Static Pod Directory

```text
/etc/kubernetes/manifests/
```

### View Control Plane Pods

```bash
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
```

### Check Kubelet Service

```bash
systemctl status kubelet
```

### Important Note

Not every Kubernetes installation runs all control-plane components as static Pods.

Managed Kubernetes services and externally managed etcd installations may use different architectures.

